WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.778
Welcome to "Share Your World."

00:00:01.778 --> 00:00:04.481
I'm Caroline and I love to travel.

00:00:04.481 --> 00:00:06.800
While I'm not able to explore right now,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.300
I'm inviting four other travel lovers

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.240
from different cultural
backgrounds to come together

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.224
and share our worlds with each other.

00:00:14.224 --> 00:00:16.659
In this episode, we're sharing a few

00:00:16.659 --> 00:00:18.528
of our favorite wedding traditions.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:28.190
Every culture has its own
ways of tying the knot.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:30.573
Here's one of my favorite
wedding traditions.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:34.330
[Caroline] I'm Armenian and one

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:37.013
of my favorite Armenian wedding traditions

00:00:37.013 --> 00:00:39.282
is when the bride and groom

00:00:39.282 --> 00:00:41.050
are finally married.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.010
When they go into their new home

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.354
that they're gonna be living in,

00:00:44.354 --> 00:00:48.910
the groom's mother usually
places a bread called lavash,

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.880
which is traditional Armenian flatbread

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.350
over the newlyweds' shoulders.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:58.680
And that's kind of to bless
them with bread and food

00:00:58.680 --> 00:00:59.469
in the house.

00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:01.604
And then one other thing, before

00:01:01.604 --> 00:01:03.370
they step into their home,

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:07.040
they have to step on and break plates.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.490
And that's kind of like
to get the bad luck out

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.382
of the house before they step in.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.419
[Kasey] In Korea, there's a tradition

00:01:16.419 --> 00:01:18.021
called a Pyebaek ceremony.

00:01:18.021 --> 00:01:19.489
So Pyebaek is when you

00:01:19.489 --> 00:01:21.390
get dressed in an old,

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.725
it's like a traditional hanbok.

00:01:22.725 --> 00:01:26.550
[Katrina]- Kasey, so what
is, you called it a hanbok?

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:27.920
Did I pronounce it correctly?

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.367
Yeah, so a hanbok is a
traditional Korean costume

00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:33.970
that they used to wear in
the palace dynasties

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:34.704
back in the day.

00:01:34.704 --> 00:01:36.100
It's silk layers.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.407
It's very colorful.

00:01:37.407 --> 00:01:39.360
All of the women have dresses.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.340
There are several skirts
that you wear underneath

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.390
and like a cute little jacket,
but it's like big sleeves

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.883
that are red, and white,
and pink, and gold.

00:01:46.883 --> 00:01:49.250
And then the guys have also similar,

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:51.688
but pants with a vest and like a hat.

00:01:51.688 --> 00:01:53.100
And it's just different layers,

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.124
but it's very silk and colorful.

00:01:55.124 --> 00:01:57.126
[Debby] Sounds beautiful, wow.

00:01:57.126 --> 00:01:58.628
[Katrina] It does sound beautiful.

00:01:58.628 --> 00:02:02.140
So my cousin, John got
married and he wore blue.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:05.350
And then my aunt and uncle
also wear blue-colored hanboks

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:06.736
because they're on the groom's side.

00:02:06.736 --> 00:02:08.830
So they'll sit down on cushions

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:10.490
and then my aunt and uncle,

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.609
the groom's parents, will throw chestnuts

00:02:12.609 --> 00:02:14.490
and they have a silk attachment

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:17.320
to her hanbok and they'll
both hold each side.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.183
And they'll try to catch as
many chestnuts as possible.

00:02:20.183 --> 00:02:23.230
And however many chestnuts you catch means

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:24.687
how many children you'll have.

00:02:25.455 --> 00:02:27.170
[Jason] How many did your cousin catch?

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:28.003
[Kasey] Three.

00:02:28.003 --> 00:02:28.836
[Jason] Three?

00:02:28.836 --> 00:02:30.710
[Kasey] Yeah, and he's one of three, so.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.237
[All] Wow.
Oh!

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.433
[Katrina] So I actually
recently got married.

00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:42.370
My traditional Nigerian,
specifically Yoruba, ceremony

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.330
was in September last year.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.150
The traditional wedding outfit for a bride

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.650
is typically the iro and buba.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:55.120
Traditionally, it's kind of
like a boxy sleeved U-necked top

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.955
that's sewn in lace or aso oke.

00:02:58.955 --> 00:03:01.990
It's a more fancy fabric
that we typically use

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:03.359
for special ceremonies.

00:03:03.359 --> 00:03:05.250
It's not just for weddings,

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.831
but typically like weddings, graduation.

00:03:08.831 --> 00:03:13.240
And then the buba is a skirt
that's wrapped around you

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:14.871
and folded over.

00:03:14.871 --> 00:03:19.340
In more modern times, you're
not limited to it being a top

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.913
and a skirt that you wrap and fold.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.260
There's just a little
bit more style in it.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:26.820
And then you also wear a gele

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.340
that's wrapped really beautifully.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.230
The bride's gele is typically wrapped

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:34.691
in a special way to make her stand out.

00:03:34.691 --> 00:03:35.808
The planning was fun.

00:03:35.808 --> 00:03:39.900
It was like 350 people, just for scale.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.510
[Everyone] Wow!
[Katrina] Yeah, and that's not even

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.780
like a big Nigerian wedding.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.730
At home, some people's weddings

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.004
in Nigeria are like 1,000 people.

00:03:48.004 --> 00:03:51.040
[Debby] I don't even
know that many people.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:57.280
[Katrina] I don't either,
but I think it leans

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.900
to the notion of 'it takes a village.'

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:04.587
So it's like a community
has built this child up.

00:04:04.587 --> 00:04:05.790
[Caroline] It reminds me of

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:08.958
when ancient Armenian
weddings would happen.

00:04:08.958 --> 00:04:11.210
It would take seven days and seven nights.

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.810
And everyone would just bring fruits

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:15.965
and whatever they can for the couple.

00:04:15.965 --> 00:04:17.886
But it was kind of like
they were all involved

00:04:17.886 --> 00:04:19.520
in this couple.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.304
They were all involved in the marriage.

00:04:25.775 --> 00:04:28.420
[Caroline] I wanna travel and
I wanna have a big wedding.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:30.230
Well not me, maybe.

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:31.691
[Kasey] Yeah, I wanna go to a wedding.

00:04:31.691 --> 00:04:34.984
[Katrina] Yeah, I wanna go to a wedding.

00:04:34.984 --> 00:04:36.870
[Kasey] So I actually
have my friend's wedding

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.060
in London next year.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:40.240
It was actually postponed from this year.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:41.624
So I'm really excited to go.

00:04:41.624 --> 00:04:42.470
What's nice is,

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:45.027
because we've been able to
travel domestically this year,

00:04:45.027 --> 00:04:47.170
\

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:49.320
and I'm able to accrue a ton of points

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.150
which I can actually use
to redeem hotel nights

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:54.303
in the future for my
friend's wedding in London.

00:04:54.303 --> 00:04:56.690
[Caroline] Let's all make a
vow to keep looking forward

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:58.690
to future weddings and trips.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:04:59.720
And in the meantime,

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:03.040
we hope our video inspires
you to learn more about these

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:06.249
and other wedding traditions
from around the world.

